WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:02.348
Voting occurs in many different ways.

2
00:00:02.349 --> 00:00:06.356
Some people check a box or punch out a circle on a piece of paper.

3
00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:09.635
Others pull a lever or touch a computer screen.

4
00:00:09.636 --> 00:00:11.921
Some simply raise their hands.

5
00:00:11.922 --> 00:00:17.341
But they all exercise one of the most important rights in the modern world: the right to vote.

6
00:00:17.342 --> 00:00:25.548
"Democracy," which means "ruled by the people," exists today because, for centuries, people have fought for the right to vote.

7
00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:31.777
In fact, it wasn't too long ago that most people had no say at all in how their countries were run.

8
00:00:31.778 --> 00:00:35.706
Yet even now, democracy remains very fragile.

9
00:00:35.707 --> 00:00:40.144
Most historians place the birthplace of democracy in the Greek city of Athens.

10
00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:49.764
In approximately 600 BC, life in Ancient Greece was hard, and few people had time to worry about anything other than putting food on the table.

11
00:00:49.765 --> 00:00:54.582
Most people were ruled by kings, who passed their power down to their sons.

12
00:00:54.583 --> 00:01:01.467
But some people grew rich enough that they were able to spend their time working out better ways to organize their communities.

13
00:01:01.468 --> 00:01:05.864
They started to demand that they be included in the decision-making process.

14
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:10.085
In this early form of democracy, many people were excluded.

15
00:01:10.086 --> 00:01:14.609
Women, slaves, and foreigners were left out of the process altogether.

16
00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.727
Only men who owned property were allowed to vote.

17
00:01:17.728 --> 00:01:23.838
These men were called "citizens" and they would meet in person to discuss their opinions and cast their votes.

18
00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:29.518
As cities got larger, it became harder to gather all the men together to vote on every subject.

19
00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:35.675
So they decided that each neighborhood would elect an individual to make decisions on behalf of their community.

20
00:01:35.676 --> 00:01:41.546
This is the system of democracy that survives to this day in the form of parliaments and legislatures.

21
00:01:41.547 --> 00:01:47.473
It is there that professional politicians, who are paid to represent their communities, pass laws.

22
00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:52.646
After the Greeks, the Ancient Romans were the next society to institute democracy.

23
00:01:52.647 --> 00:01:57.369
They adopted the democracy of the Greek cities to the entire Roman Empire.

24
00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:02.587
Roman democracy gave soldiers, as well as property-owning men, the right to vote.

25
00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:13.643
But after a few hundred years, soldiers had elected so many military officers to public office that the military representatives regularly outvoted non-military representatives.

26
00:02:13.644 --> 00:02:22.962
In 40 BC, the politicians loyal to the military decided to dissolve the democratic system and replace it with a dictatorship under Julius Caesar.

27
00:02:22.963 --> 00:02:29.997
Emperors ruled Rome for the next four hundred years, and under their control, the empire slowly fell apart.

28
00:02:29.998 --> 00:02:38.648
For the next thousand years after the collapse of the Roman Empire, the notion of democracy was scarcely ever discussed, let alone practiced.

29
00:02:38.649 --> 00:02:46.037
In the 1450s, a revival of democratic ideas began, largely thanks to the invention of the printing press.

30
00:02:46.038 --> 00:02:51.535
This revolutionary technology allowed people to print books and newspapers quickly and cheaply.

31
00:02:51.536 --> 00:02:56.328
In Europe, people started to read about the old Greek cities and the rights of citizens.

32
00:02:56.329 --> 00:03:02.595
In the 1500s, a group in Spain called the Comuneros tried to put this idea into practice.

33
00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:07.635
Villages elected people to represent them at regional meetings to work out common problems.

34
00:03:07.636 --> 00:03:13.758
The king of Spain, Charles V, did not welcome the trend, and he had his soldiers stamp out the movement.

35
00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:18.258
Over the next two hundred years, democratic ideas continued to spread.

36
00:03:18.259 --> 00:03:26.789
British and French writers like John Stuart Mill, John Locke, and Jean-Jacques Rousseau developed a new philosophy based on democracy.

37
00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:33.352
They believed that every person is created equal and that everyone should have an equal say in how government works.

38
00:03:33.353 --> 00:03:39.728
In 1689, based on the ideas of these thinkers, England produced the first Bill of Rights.

39
00:03:39.729 --> 00:03:46.950
This document included the right of English men to elect representatives to a parliament, which made the laws of the country.

40
00:03:46.951 --> 00:03:54.342
In 1215, an earlier document called the Magna Carta had compelled English kings to respect those laws.

41
00:03:54.343 --> 00:04:02.374
Thus, even though England still had a king, the representatives elected to parliament had an important role in how the people were governed.

42
00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:06.580
As in Ancient Greece, most people weren't allowed to vote.

43
00:04:06.581 --> 00:04:13.227
Only a few wealthy landowners, who pledged to consider the needs of everyone else, had the right to vote.

44
00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:19.116
The next major step in the development of democracy took place in the British colonies in North America.

45
00:04:19.117 --> 00:04:27.802
Many British subjects in these colonies resented paying taxes to the English king because they weren't able to elect representatives to the English parliament.

46
00:04:27.803 --> 00:04:32.133
In 1776, the colonies declared independence.

47
00:04:32.134 --> 00:04:37.133
This led to the American Revolution, a war the colonists eventually won.

48
00:04:37.134 --> 00:04:41.777
The new alliance of 13 colonies called itself the United States of America.

49
00:04:41.778 --> 00:04:49.007
The founders of this new country included Thomas Jefferson, Benjamin Franklin, George Washington, and others.

50
00:04:49.008 --> 00:04:55.109
These thinkers adopted several of the ideals of the philosophers who created the English Bill of Rights.

51
00:04:55.110 --> 00:05:02.925
They developed a government that separated the church from the state, meaning that religion should not affect laws and how the government is run.

52
00:05:02.926 --> 00:05:06.559
They also divided political power among several groups.

53
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:17.084
These measures were designed to ensure that no one group gained too much control, as had happened in Rome when the military grew so dominant that it abolished democracy altogether.

54
00:05:17.085 --> 00:05:24.035
Soon after the United States won its independence, revolutionaries in France launched their own democratic rebellion.

55
00:05:24.036 --> 00:05:31.591
The French Revolution, one of the more famous events in the development of democracy, involved a violent overthrow of the monarchy.

56
00:05:31.592 --> 00:05:42.102
Peaceful conversions to democracy followed elsewhere, including in Canada in 1867, Australia in 1901, and Sweden in 1917.

57
00:05:42.103 --> 00:05:47.299
Soon, all the countries of Europe were under various forms of democratic government.

58
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:56.471
In many countries, the restriction that citizens must own land was lifted; however, in most cases, the right to vote was still limited to white men.

59
00:05:56.472 --> 00:06:04.947
In the United States, after the Civil War in the 1860s, racial restrictions were lifted, so that some non-white men could vote.

60
00:06:04.948 --> 00:06:10.892
Women got the vote in Canada in 1918, and two years later, in the United States.

61
00:06:10.893 --> 00:06:21.028
In Britain, women over the age of thirty won the right to vote in nation-wide elections in 1918, but all women didn't win the right to vote until 1928.

62
00:06:21.029 --> 00:06:25.425
Democracy has improved the lives of many, but it also has pitfalls.

63
00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:34.800
Often, people who had the vote didn't want others to have it because they were afraid they would lose their power, which led to conflicts and sometimes even wars.

64
00:06:34.801 --> 00:06:38.074
Sometimes, people have actually lost the right to vote.

65
00:06:38.075 --> 00:06:44.144
In France, for example, the first democratic government was dominated by power-hungry politicians.

66
00:06:44.145 --> 00:06:49.606
A few years after France's democracy was born, the country slipped back into a dictatorship.

67
00:06:49.607 --> 00:06:52.915
It took decades for France to reestablish democracy.

68
00:06:52.916 --> 00:06:55.701
People have even voted their way out of democracy.

69
00:06:55.702 --> 00:07:02.608
In Germany, a young democratic government fell apart when Adolf Hitler was elected chancellor in 1933.

70
00:07:02.609 --> 00:07:07.011
Hitler quickly began to change the government into a military dictatorship.

71
00:07:07.012 --> 00:07:13.446
In the 1990s, the communist system of the Soviet Union collapsed and was replaced by democracy.

72
00:07:13.447 --> 00:07:17.812
But it was a form of democracy that gave too much power to wealthy people.

73
00:07:17.813 --> 00:07:23.274
Living conditions declined, and for a while, support for the old system grew again.

74
00:07:23.275 --> 00:07:28.100
Even the United States, considered the flag-bearer of democracy, has problems.

75
00:07:28.101 --> 00:07:31.928
Voting rates in the U.S. are among the lowest in modern democracies.

76
00:07:31.929 --> 00:07:37.579
Rarely do more than half of those with the right to vote bother to exercise it in a national election.

77
00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:46.197
Perhaps former British Prime Minister Winston Churchill summed it up best when he called democracy the worst form of government─except for all the others.

78
00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:50.151
By its very nature, democracy is a fragile form of government.

79
00:07:50.152 --> 00:07:59.248
Its fragility and its strength lie in the same complicated feature: the need for a great many people to be involved, protecting the rights of all.

